  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 235 AM CDT Wed Sep 16 2026\n\nShowers and storms look to persist through the night as shortwaves \ncirculating around the SE CONUS upper level high continue to pass \nover the region. CAM guidance indicates a somewhat disheveled LLJ \nforming across SE KS and western MO. This has resulted in slightly \nlower than expected rain/storm coverage through the overnight which \ntampers rain expectations slightly tampering flood concerns. With \nmore sparse coverage of rain, areas will see opportunities for water \nto drain before the next round moves in. That said, the overall \nenvironment remains conducive for isolated heavy downpours which may \nresult in localized flooding across the area.\n\nThe primary facilitator of this activity is a shallow profiled \nsurface cold front which moved through the area Tuesday evening. As \nof midnight, the surface front sit just southeast of our CWA \nstretching from approximately central IL through Jefferson City, MO \nsouthwest through Pittsburg, KS. The midlevel front, by comparison, \nresides somewhat across far SE Nebraska, NW MO, and into southern \nIA. This has created an expansive area of isentropic ascent enhanced \nby the passing upper level shortwaves mentioned previously. Closer \nto sunrise, the boundaries are expected to retreat back northward as \na warm front keeping rain chances around, but gradually moving \nnorthward through the morning hours. Rain should exit the region by \nmid morning for those west of the I-35 corridor and early afternoon \nfor those east.\n\nWith the boundary moving northward, temperatures do start trending \nwarmer. Like Tuesday, a dichotomy of temperatures is expected with \nrain cooled areas (mainly NW MO) ranging from the mid 70s to mid 80s \nwith low to upper 90s expected everywhere else. Who sees lower vs \nhigh temperatures will depend on how far north the boundary makes it \nthrough the day. Right now, the forecast calls for the boundary to \nmake it to the MO River Valley; however, there is a chance that the \nboundary could advance further north bringing hotter air further \nnorthward. Heat indicies south of the temperature boundary are \nexpected to be around 100-105F.\n\nThe upper level weather pattern continues to be dominated by a ridge \nacross the SE CONUS and a trough digging across the Pacific NW. \nEastern KS and much of MO find itself in the traditional ring of fire \npattern on the periphery of the expansive SE CONUS high which has \nbeen providing at least some of the catalyst for the showers and \nstorms seen yesterday into this morning. Thursday, the Pacific NW \nlow digs a little further south which starts building a pattern of \nejecting leeward trough through the central CONUS. Combine this with \nthe ring of fire pattern mentioned earlier and the generally active \npattern across the region looks more likely to continue. The first \nof this starts Thursday morning as a brief wave of showers and \nstorms looks to pass through far NW MO during the morning. As \nThursday becomes Friday, the pressure gradient across the central \nCONUS contracts as the two systems battle for real estate. Lower \nlevel dynamics look to be a bit more action packed with multiple lee \ntroughs taking advantage of the increased flow. There remains some \nuncertainty in the track of the system as steering current from \nwinds aloft remain somewhat variable, but overall consensus points \nto another opportunity for rain across the area Saturday through \nMonday. It remains a bit far out to pinpoint fine details, but the \nbroad scale dynamics show another setup for a slow moving synoptic \ncold front that struggles to proceed southeastward which could \npresent another favorable setup for prolonged rainfall. Extended \nguidance shows flow opening up with systems becoming much more \ntransitive mid to late next week.",
